module.exports = {
  title: 'Vue Fundamentals',
  slug: 'fundamentals',
  order: 0,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'What Vue Is and Where It Fits',
      slug: 'introduction-to-vue',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Almost every Vue interview opens with some version of "so, why Vue?" It sounds like small talk. It is not. The interviewer is checking whether you can place Vue on the map of front-end tools — whether you understand what problem it solves, what it deliberately does <em>not</em> solve, and what you give up by choosing it. A candidate who answers "it's easier than React" has told the interviewer they have never had to defend a framework choice to a team. A candidate who can explain Vue's <strong>progressive</strong> design, its compile-time advantages and its trade-offs against React and Angular has demonstrated, in ninety seconds, that they think about engineering decisions rather than just following tutorials.</p>`,
        `<p>This matters practically too. Vue's core promise — that you can adopt it incrementally, one page or one widget at a time — is the reason it wins in a specific category of project: an existing server-rendered application (Rails, Laravel, Django, ASP.NET) that needs interactive islands without a full rewrite. If you cannot articulate that, you cannot tell your team when Vue is the right call.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue is a <strong>JavaScript framework for building user interfaces</strong>. Its central idea is the same one behind every modern UI framework: you describe what the screen should look like <em>for a given state</em>, and the framework takes responsibility for making the real DOM match that description whenever the state changes. You stop writing "find this element, change its text, toggle that class" and start writing "the heading is the user's name" — a fact that stays true as the name changes.</p>`,
        `<p>Two words are used constantly to describe Vue, and both are load-bearing:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Declarative.</strong> You declare the relationship between state and DOM once. Vue re-establishes it on every change. The alternative — imperative DOM manipulation, as in jQuery — makes you re-state that relationship at every call site, which is why imperative UI code rots as features are added.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Progressive.</strong> Vue is a layered framework. The runtime alone is a script tag you can drop onto an existing HTML page. Add the router when you need client-side navigation, Pinia when you need shared state, the build tooling when you need single-file components. Nothing forces you to take all of it on day one.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch1-progressive-layers.svg|Concentric layers showing Vue's progressive adoption: core runtime at the centre, then components and single-file components, then router and Pinia, then build tooling and SSR|Vue is adopted in layers — each ring is optional until the application actually needs it.}}`,
        `<p>The unit of construction is the <strong>component</strong>: a self-contained piece of UI that owns its own template, its own logic and its own styles. A page is a tree of components. This is not unique to Vue — it is the shared vocabulary of the entire modern front end — but Vue's <strong>Single-File Component</strong> (<code>.vue</code>) is a distinctive expression of it, keeping all three concerns in one file rather than three.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>To understand what makes Vue Vue, it helps to see the three pieces that run underneath every application.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>1. The reactivity system.</strong> Vue tracks which pieces of state each part of your UI actually read. When a piece of state changes, Vue knows precisely which components depend on it and re-renders only those. In Vue 3 this is built on JavaScript <code>Proxy</code>, which lets Vue intercept property reads and writes on plain objects. This is the single biggest architectural difference from React: React re-renders a component and its children whenever state changes and relies on you to prevent the waste (<code>memo</code>, <code>useMemo</code>, <code>useCallback</code>); Vue's dependency tracking makes that bookkeeping largely unnecessary. It is the subject of Chapter 4 and the deepest well of interview questions in the framework.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>2. The compiler.</strong> Vue templates are not interpreted at runtime — they are compiled to render functions ahead of time by <code>@vue/compiler-sfc</code> during your build. Because the template is a <em>static, analysable</em> structure (unlike JSX, which is arbitrary JavaScript), the compiler can prove things about it. It can see that a <code>&lt;div class="card"&gt;</code> with no bindings can never change and hoist it out of the render function entirely. It can mark exactly which attributes on an element are dynamic with a <em>patch flag</em>, so the runtime skips diffing everything else. This "compiler-informed virtual DOM" is why Vue 3 gets React-like ergonomics at materially better runtime performance, and it is a genuinely strong answer to "why Vue over React?"</p>`,
        `<p><strong>3. The renderer.</strong> Vue maintains a virtual DOM — a lightweight JavaScript description of the intended UI — diffs the new description against the old one, and applies the minimum set of real DOM operations. Guided by the compiler's patch flags, that diff is far cheaper than a naive tree walk.</p>`,
        `<p>The practical shape of all this is the Single-File Component. One file, three blocks, each doing one job:</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Counter.vue — a Single-File Component --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\n\nconst count = ref(0)                          // reactive state\nconst doubled = computed(() =&gt; count.value * 2) // derived state\n\nfunction increment() {\n  count.value++                               // .value in script, not in template\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;button @click="increment"&gt;\n    Clicked {{ count }} times (doubled: {{ doubled }})\n  &lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;style scoped&gt;\nbutton { padding: 0.5rem 1rem; }  /* scoped — cannot leak to other components */\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>That file is the whole component: state, behaviour, markup and styles. Note what is absent — no manual DOM query, no event-listener cleanup, no instruction to re-render. You changed <code>count</code>; the button's text is now stale; Vue's job is to fix that, not yours.</p>`,
        `<p>The progressive claim is not marketing. Here is Vue with no build step at all, running on a plain HTML page:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;div id="app"&gt;\n  &lt;button @click="count++"&gt;Clicked {{ count }} times&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n&lt;script src="https://unpkg.com/vue@3/dist/vue.global.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\n  const { createApp, ref } = Vue\n  createApp({\n    setup() {\n      return { count: ref(0) }\n    },\n  }).mount('#app')\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The same component model, no toolchain. This is what makes Vue the pragmatic choice for adding interactivity to a server-rendered app: you enhance one <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and leave the rest of the page alone.</p>`,
        `<p>Where does Vue sit against its two main rivals? Interviewers ask this constantly, and the honest answer is a table, not a winner:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Vue</th><th>React</th><th>Angular</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Progressive framework — core is view-only, official router/store</td><td>Library — view only, ecosystem picks the rest</td><td>Full platform — router, HTTP, forms, DI included</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>UI is written as</td><td>Templates (compiled, analysable); JSX optional</td><td>JSX — arbitrary JavaScript</td><td>Templates with Angular-specific syntax</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Reactivity</td><td>Proxy-based, fine-grained dependency tracking</td><td>Re-render on state change; manual memoisation</td><td>Signals (v16+) / Zone.js change detection</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Re-render granularity</td><td>Only components whose dependencies changed</td><td>Component subtree, unless memoised</td><td>Component tree, guided by change-detection strategy</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>State management</td><td>Pinia (official)</td><td>Redux / Zustand / Context — you choose</td><td>NgRx / signals / services</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Language</td><td>JS or TS, both first-class</td><td>JS or TS</td><td>TypeScript, effectively mandatory</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Learning curve</td><td>Gentle — HTML-like templates</td><td>Moderate — JSX plus hook rules</td><td>Steep — DI, RxJS, decorators up front</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Sweet spot</td><td>Incremental adoption; small-to-large SPAs</td><td>Large teams, huge ecosystem, React Native</td><td>Large enterprise apps wanting one prescribed way</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling Vue "a library like React".</strong> Vue ships an officially maintained router and store and treats them as part of the story; React deliberately does not. The distinction is real and interviewers listen for it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming Vue 2 knowledge transfers wholesale.</strong> Vue 3 rewrote reactivity (<code>Object.defineProperty</code> → <code>Proxy</code>), added the Composition API, and dropped filters and the multiple-root-node restriction. Answering a Vue 3 question with a Vue 2 mental model is the most common way to fail these interviews.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Believing the virtual DOM is what makes Vue fast.</strong> A virtual DOM is a cost, paid to buy a declarative programming model. Vue's speed comes from the compiler minimising that cost and from reactivity narrowing what needs to be re-rendered at all.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Treating "progressive" as a slogan.</strong> It has a concrete meaning: usable from a CDN script tag with no build step, all the way up to a full SSR application, with the same component model throughout.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for Vue reflexively when the page is static.</strong> A content page with no interactivity does not need a client-side framework; shipping one costs your users bytes and parse time for nothing.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Default to <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> Single-File Components for new work — it is the officially recommended style and the least verbose.</li>`,
        `<li>Choose Vue when incremental adoption matters, when the team is stronger in HTML/CSS than in JavaScript abstractions, or when you want an official answer for routing and state rather than a committee decision.</li>`,
        `<li>Choose deliberately, not by habit: React's ecosystem and hiring pool are larger; Angular's prescriptiveness genuinely helps very large teams. Being able to say when <em>not</em> to pick Vue is what makes your recommendation credible.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the official toolchain (<code>npm create vue@latest</code>, Vite) rather than assembling your own — it wires TypeScript, the router, Pinia, ESLint and Vitest in a configuration the ecosystem tests against.</li>`,
        `<li>Learn the reactivity system properly and early. Nearly every hard Vue interview question, and nearly every hard Vue bug, reduces to it.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is Vue and what problem does it solve?"</strong><br>A: A progressive JavaScript framework for building UIs declaratively. You describe what the DOM should look like for a given state; Vue keeps the real DOM in sync as that state changes, so you never write imperative DOM updates. "Progressive" means it is layered — a script tag on an existing page at one end, a full SSR application with router, store and build pipeline at the other, with the same component model throughout.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Vue vs React — what actually differs?"</strong><br>A: Three things that matter. First, templates vs JSX: Vue templates are statically analysable, so the compiler can hoist static content and emit patch flags telling the runtime exactly which bindings can change. React's JSX is arbitrary JavaScript and cannot be optimised that way. Second, reactivity: Vue tracks dependencies per-property via Proxy and re-renders only affected components, whereas React re-renders a subtree and leaves the memoisation to you. Third, scope: Vue ships an official router and store; React leaves those to the ecosystem. React's counter-advantages are a much larger ecosystem, a bigger hiring pool and React Native.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Vue vs Angular?"</strong><br>A: Angular is a full platform — DI, RxJS, forms, HTTP and a strong opinion about how to use them — which pays off on very large, long-lived enterprise codebases and costs a steep learning curve up front. Vue is lighter and progressive: fewer concepts to learn, faster to adopt incrementally, official-but-optional router and store. Pick Angular when you want one prescribed way for a hundred developers; pick Vue when you want to move quickly or adopt gradually.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What makes Vue 3 faster than Vue 2?" (trap — most candidates say 'virtual DOM')</strong><br>A: Not the virtual DOM — Vue 2 had one too. Three real reasons: Proxy-based reactivity that tracks additions, deletions, arrays and Maps without the <code>Vue.set</code> workarounds and without walking the whole object up front; the compiler-informed renderer, which hoists static nodes and attaches patch flags so the diff skips everything that provably cannot change; and a tree-shakeable runtime, so features you never import are not in your bundle.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When would you not choose Vue?"</strong><br>A: When the page needs no interactivity at all — ship HTML. When the team is deep in React and the hiring market matters more than the marginal technical fit. When you need React Native specifically. And on very large enterprise codebases where Angular's prescriptiveness genuinely reduces the number of arguments a big team has to have.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Vue = <strong>progressive</strong>, <strong>declarative</strong> framework for building UIs from components.</li>`,
        `<li>Progressive = CDN script tag → SFCs → router/Pinia → build tooling → SSR, same model throughout.</li>`,
        `<li>Three internals: <strong>reactivity</strong> (Proxy, fine-grained), <strong>compiler</strong> (static hoisting + patch flags), <strong>renderer</strong> (virtual DOM diff).</li>`,
        `<li>SFC = one <code>.vue</code> file with <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;style scoped&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>vs React: templates over JSX, fine-grained reactivity over subtree re-render, official router/store.</li>`,
        `<li>vs Angular: lighter and progressive vs full prescribed platform.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue 3 &gt; Vue 2 because of Proxy reactivity, compiler optimisation and tree-shaking — <em>not</em> the virtual DOM.</li>`,
        `<li>Virtual DOM is a cost you pay for declarativeness, not a performance feature.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Creating an App: Instance, Mounting and Configuration',
      slug: 'app-instance-mounting',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Every Vue application begins with one call — <code>createApp()</code> — and ends with one — <code>.mount()</code>. Between them sits the application's entire global configuration: which plugins are installed, which components are available everywhere, how uncaught errors are handled. Interviewers use this topic to separate people who have <em>configured</em> a Vue application from people who have only edited components inside one, because the app instance is where the framework-level decisions live.</p>`,
        `<p>It is also the source of a whole class of confusing bugs. Registering a plugin after mounting silently does nothing. Mounting onto a selector that does not exist yet fails quietly. Two <code>createApp()</code> calls on one page do not share a router or a store, and the resulting "why is my Pinia state empty?" is a genuinely hard bug to see. Understanding the instance makes all of these obvious rather than mysterious.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>An <strong>application instance</strong> is a self-contained Vue world. It has a root component, its own global registrations, its own plugin list, and — once mounted — its own region of the DOM that it owns. Vue 3 made this an explicit object, which is one of its most important departures from Vue 2.</p>`,
        `<p>In Vue 2, configuration was global to the <em>library</em>: <code>Vue.use(...)</code>, <code>Vue.component(...)</code>, <code>Vue.prototype.$http = ...</code> applied to every Vue instance in the page. Two apps on one page could not have different plugins, and unit tests polluted each other because there was no way to un-register. In Vue 3, all of that hangs off the object <code>createApp()</code> returns, so two apps on one page are genuinely independent.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch1-app-instance.svg|Diagram of createApp returning an app instance with config, component, directive, use and mount methods, mounting a root component into a DOM container|createApp builds an isolated application instance; everything configured on it applies only to that instance's component tree.}}`,
        `<p>The lifecycle of that object has a hard boundary in the middle of it:</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li><strong>Create</strong> — <code>createApp(RootComponent)</code> builds the instance. Nothing is rendered yet.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Configure</strong> — install plugins with <code>.use()</code>, register global components and directives, set <code>app.config</code>, provide app-level values. All of this <em>must</em> happen now.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mount</strong> — <code>.mount('#app')</code> renders the root component into the container and returns the root component instance (not the app).</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `<p>After step 3, configuration calls are ignored. That is the rule most people learn the hard way.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><code>createApp()</code> returns an object exposing a small, deliberately chainable API. Each method returns the app, so configuration reads as a pipeline:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>app.use(plugin, options)</code> — installs a plugin. A plugin is anything with an <code>install(app, options)</code> method (or a plain function taking the same arguments). Vue Router and Pinia are both plugins; so is your own <code>i18n</code> wrapper. Installing the same plugin twice is a no-op, which makes double-installation harmless rather than corrupting.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.component(name, definition)</code> — registers a component available in <em>every</em> template in this app without importing it.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.directive(name, definition)</code> — the same for custom directives.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.provide(key, value)</code> — supplies a value that any descendant can <code>inject()</code>, no matter how deep. This is the app-level entry point to the dependency-injection system covered in Chapter 2.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.config.errorHandler</code> — a single function receiving every uncaught error from every component in the app, with the component instance and a string describing where it came from. This is where you wire Sentry.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.config.globalProperties.$x</code> — the Vue 3 replacement for <code>Vue.prototype.$x</code>. It works, but it is an escape hatch; <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> or a plain import is almost always better because it is typed and traceable.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.mount(container)</code> — attaches to a CSS selector or a real element. Vue replaces the container's inner content with the rendered root component.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.unmount()</code> — tears the whole thing down, running every component's unmount hooks. Essential when Vue is an island inside a page owned by something else.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>Two subtleties are worth internalising. First, <code>mount()</code> returns the <strong>root component instance</strong>, not the app — so <code>createApp(App).mount('#app').use(router)</code> is a type error waiting to happen, and it is exactly the shape of the "my router does nothing" bug. Second, the container element must exist when you call <code>mount()</code>. In a normal SPA the script runs after the container is in <code>index.html</code>, so this is invisible — but embed Vue in a server-rendered page, or mount from a <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> script, and it bites immediately.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// main.js — the standard entry point of a Vue 3 SPA\nimport { createApp } from 'vue'\nimport App from './App.vue'\nimport router from './router'\nimport { createPinia } from 'pinia'\nimport BaseButton from './components/BaseButton.vue'\nimport './assets/main.css'\n\nconst app = createApp(App)\n\n// ── configure, all BEFORE mount ──\napp.use(createPinia())          // state management\napp.use(router)                 // client-side routing\napp.component('BaseButton', BaseButton)  // usable in any template, no import\napp.provide('apiBaseUrl', import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL)\n\napp.config.errorHandler = (err, instance, info) =&gt; {\n  // info describes the hook/lifecycle the error came from\n  reportToSentry(err, { component: instance?.$options.name, info })\n}\n\napp.mount('#app')              // ← after this line, configuration is closed</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Chained, the same thing reads as a single expression — and shows the trap clearly:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ✅ Fine — mount() is last\ncreateApp(App).use(createPinia()).use(router).mount('#app')\n\n// ❌ Broken — .use() runs on the ROOT COMPONENT INSTANCE returned by mount(),\n//    not on the app. Router is never installed; every &lt;RouterView&gt; is empty.\ncreateApp(App).mount('#app').use(router)</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Multiple independent apps on one page — the "Vue as progressive enhancement" case:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// A server-rendered page with two interactive islands.\n// Separate instances: separate plugins, separate global components, separate state.\nimport { createApp } from 'vue'\nimport SearchWidget from './SearchWidget.vue'\nimport CartWidget from './CartWidget.vue'\n\ncreateApp(SearchWidget).mount('#search')\ncreateApp(CartWidget).use(createPinia()).mount('#cart')\n\n// ⚠️ These two do NOT share Pinia state — each createApp() has its own\n// plugin registry. If they must share, mount ONE app that renders both.</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Mounting into a page you don't own — check the container exists,\n// and keep the handle so you can clean up.\nconst el = document.querySelector('#legacy-slot')\nif (el) {\n  const app = createApp(Widget)\n  app.mount(el)\n  // Later, when the host page tears the section down:\n  //   app.unmount()  ← runs every child's onUnmounted, clears listeners/timers\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Configuring after mounting.</strong> <code>app.use()</code>, <code>app.component()</code> and <code>app.provide()</code> after <code>mount()</code> are silently ignored. No error, no warning in production — just a feature that does nothing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Chaining <code>.use()</code> after <code>.mount()</code>.</strong> The same bug wearing a disguise, because <code>mount()</code> returns the root component instance rather than the app.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting two <code>createApp()</code> calls to share state.</strong> They are isolated worlds. Two islands that need shared state must live in one app, or share through something outside Vue.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mounting before the container exists.</strong> Vue warns and renders nothing. Common when the script tag sits above the target element, or the container is injected by a CMS after page load.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for <code>globalProperties</code> as the default sharing mechanism.</strong> It is untyped, invisible to your IDE and hostile to tree-shaking. Prefer a plain module import, or <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> when the value must vary per app.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>unmount()</code> for embedded widgets.</strong> Without it, watchers, timers and event listeners inside the widget outlive the DOM node — a real memory leak in long-lived pages.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Carrying Vue 2 habits over.</strong> <code>Vue.use()</code>, <code>Vue.component()</code> and <code>Vue.prototype</code> no longer exist. Everything is per-app now, which is the point.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Keep <code>main.js</code> as the single, boring place where the app is assembled: create, configure, mount, in that order and nothing else.</li>`,
        `<li>Register a component globally only if it genuinely appears nearly everywhere (design-system primitives). Global registration defeats tree-shaking and hides a component's dependencies from the file that uses it.</li>`,
        `<li>Always set <code>app.config.errorHandler</code> in production — without it, component errors reach the console and nowhere else.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer plain ES module imports for shared utilities; use <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> when a value is genuinely app-scoped (an API base URL, a theme, a test double).</li>`,
        `<li>Hold on to the app instance if the surrounding page can remove your mount point, and call <code>unmount()</code> when it does.</li>`,
        `<li>Bootstrap new projects with <code>npm create vue@latest</code> rather than hand-rolling the entry file — it produces this structure with TypeScript, router, Pinia and Vitest already wired.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Walk me through what happens between createApp and the first paint."</strong><br>A: <code>createApp(App)</code> builds an isolated application instance with its own plugin, component and directive registries — nothing renders yet. You then configure it: <code>use()</code> for plugins, <code>component()</code>/<code>directive()</code> for globals, <code>provide()</code> for injectables, <code>config.errorHandler</code> for reporting. <code>mount('#app')</code> then creates the root component instance, runs its setup, compiles/executes the render function to a virtual DOM tree, and patches that into the container. Configuration after mount is ignored.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why did Vue 3 replace the global <code>Vue.use()</code> with a per-app instance?"</strong><br>A: Global configuration made isolation impossible. In Vue 2 every plugin, global component and <code>Vue.prototype</code> extension applied to every instance on the page, so two apps could not differ, unit tests leaked configuration into one another, and there was no clean teardown. Making the app an object scopes all of it and makes multiple independent apps genuinely independent.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Someone reports the router does nothing — <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> is always empty. Where do you look?" (trap)</strong><br>A: The entry file, for order. Almost always it is <code>createApp(App).mount('#app').use(router)</code>: <code>mount()</code> returns the root component instance, not the app, so <code>use()</code> installs the router on the wrong object and fails silently. Move every <code>use()</code> before <code>mount()</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Two Vue apps on one page — do they share a Pinia store?"</strong><br>A: No. Plugins are registered per app instance, so <code>createPinia()</code> installed on one app is invisible to the other; even the same Pinia object installed on both would give you two separate app contexts. If two widgets must share state, render both from a single app, or move the shared state outside Vue entirely.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is <code>app.config.globalProperties</code> and should you use it?"</strong><br>A: The Vue 3 stand-in for <code>Vue.prototype</code> — it attaches a property reachable as <code>this.$x</code> in every component. It exists mainly to ease Vue 2 migration. Avoid it in new code: it is untyped without extra declaration merging, invisible to editors and tree-shakers, and creates hidden coupling. Import the module, or provide/inject.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>createApp(Root)</code> → configure → <code>mount(container)</code>. Order is non-negotiable.</li>`,
        `<li><code>mount()</code> returns the <strong>root component instance</strong>, not the app — never chain <code>.use()</code> after it.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.use()</code> plugins · <code>app.component()</code> globals · <code>app.directive()</code> · <code>app.provide()</code> · <code>app.config.errorHandler</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>A plugin is any object with <code>install(app, options)</code>. Installing twice is a no-op.</li>`,
        `<li>Configuration after <code>mount()</code> is <strong>silently ignored</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Each <code>createApp()</code> is an isolated world — no shared plugins, no shared store.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.unmount()</code> tears down cleanly; use it for widgets embedded in pages you don't control.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue 2's <code>Vue.use</code> / <code>Vue.component</code> / <code>Vue.prototype</code> → Vue 3's per-app equivalents.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Template Syntax and Interpolation',
      slug: 'template-syntax',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>The template is where most Vue code actually lives, and it is the surface interviewers probe first because misunderstandings there are so visible. Why does <code>{{ }}</code> work for text but not for an attribute? Why does <code>v-bind</code> exist when interpolation already does substitution? Why can you write an expression but not a statement? Every one of those questions has a precise answer rooted in how the template compiler works, and candidates who know it write templates that stay readable as the app grows.</p>`,
        `<p>There is also a security dimension. <code>{{ }}</code> escapes HTML; <code>v-html</code> does not. Knowing which is which — and why the difference exists — is the difference between a safe app and a cross-site-scripting hole, and it is asked in interviews precisely because it is so easy to get wrong under deadline pressure.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A Vue template is <strong>valid HTML with extra attributes</strong>. That is a deliberate design choice: any browser can parse it, any HTML tool understands it, and a designer can read it. Vue adds exactly two kinds of dynamic syntax on top.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>1. Text interpolation</strong> — the "mustache" <code>{{ expression }}</code>. It substitutes a value into <em>text content</em>. The value is converted to a string and HTML-escaped, so <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> arrives as visible characters rather than executable markup.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>2. Directives</strong> — special attributes beginning with <code>v-</code>. Where mustaches handle text, directives handle everything else: attributes (<code>v-bind</code>), events (<code>v-on</code>), conditional rendering (<code>v-if</code>), lists (<code>v-for</code>), and more. A directive's job is to apply reactive behaviour to the element it sits on.</p>`,
        `<p>The two most common directives have shorthands you will see everywhere and should use:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Full form</th><th>Shorthand</th><th>Does what</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-bind:href="url"</code></td><td><code>:href="url"</code></td><td>Binds an attribute/prop to an expression</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-on:click="fn"</code></td><td><code>@click="fn"</code></td><td>Attaches an event listener</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-bind="obj"</code></td><td><code>v-bind="obj"</code></td><td>Spreads every key of an object as attributes</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>v-slot:name</code></td><td><code>#name</code></td><td>Targets a named slot</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The rule that explains most confusion: <strong>mustaches only work in text position.</strong> <code>&lt;a href="{{ url }}"&gt;</code> is not valid Vue — inside an attribute you must use <code>v-bind</code>. The reason is structural, and it is the next section.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Templates are <strong>compiled, not interpreted</strong>. During your build, <code>@vue/compiler-sfc</code> parses the template into an Abstract Syntax Tree, optimises it, and emits a JavaScript <em>render function</em> that returns a virtual DOM tree. The browser never sees your template — it sees the compiled output.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch1-template-compilation.svg|Pipeline diagram showing a Vue template parsed into an AST, transformed with static hoisting and patch flags, then generated into a render function returning virtual DOM|Templates are compiled ahead of time into render functions — which is what lets Vue hoist static content and flag exactly which bindings can change.}}`,
        `<p>That compile step is why mustaches and <code>v-bind</code> are different things. The compiler walks the parsed HTML: text nodes containing <code>{{ }}</code> become calls that produce dynamic text children, while attributes are a different part of the node structure entirely and need a directive to become dynamic. Once you see the template as a tree being transformed rather than a string being search-and-replaced, the rule stops feeling arbitrary.</p>`,
        `<p>Compilation is also where Vue's performance advantage is built. The compiler can see which parts of the tree contain no bindings at all and <strong>hoist</strong> them out of the render function so they are created once and reused on every render. For nodes that do have bindings, it emits a <strong>patch flag</strong> — a bitmask recording exactly which aspects are dynamic (text only, class only, props, and so on) — so the runtime diff can skip everything else. React cannot do this, because JSX is arbitrary JavaScript and cannot be statically analysed the same way.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>What may go inside the braces?</strong> A <em>single JavaScript expression</em>, evaluated in a sandbox whose scope is the component instance plus a small allow-list of globals (<code>Math</code>, <code>Date</code>, <code>JSON</code>…). Expressions evaluate to a value; statements do not. So:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>✅ <code>{{ count + 1 }}</code>, <code>{{ ok ? 'Yes' : 'No' }}</code>, <code>{{ msg.split('').reverse().join('') }}</code></li>`,
        `<li>❌ <code>{{ const x = 1 }}</code> — a declaration, not an expression</li>`,
        `<li>❌ <code>{{ if (ok) { return 'yes' } }}</code> — a statement; use a ternary</li>`,
        `<li>❌ <code>{{ window.location.href }}</code> — <code>window</code> is not in the allow-list; expose it from the component if you need it</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>Note that <code>ref</code>s are <strong>unwrapped</strong> in templates: you write <code>{{ count }}</code>, not <code>{{ count.value }}</code>, even though the script side needs <code>.value</code>. Vue unwraps top-level refs returned from <code>setup</code> as a convenience. This is a favourite trick question and is covered in depth in Chapter 4.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Escaping and <code>v-html</code>.</strong> Mustaches escape their output — that is not a formatting detail, it is the framework's built-in XSS defence. <code>v-html</code> deliberately bypasses it and injects raw markup. If the string came from a user, from a URL, or from an API you do not control, <code>v-html</code> is a stored-XSS vulnerability. Chapter 9 covers the sanitisation story; the rule to memorise now is <em>never <code>v-html</code> untrusted content</em>.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\n\nconst user = ref({ name: 'Ada', id: 42 })\nconst isActive = ref(true)\nconst rawHtml = ref('&lt;em&gt;from the CMS&lt;/em&gt;')\nconst attrs = ref({ id: 'profile', 'data-testid': 'user-card' })\nconst initials = computed(() =&gt; user.value.name.slice(0, 2).toUpperCase())\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- 1. Text interpolation — escaped, expression only --&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hello, {{ user.name }}&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ initials }} · id {{ user.id }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ isActive ? 'Active' : 'Inactive' }}&lt;/p&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 2. Attribute binding — mustaches DON'T work here --&gt;\n  &lt;a :href="\`/users/\${user.id}\`"&gt;Profile&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;img :src="user.avatar" :alt="\`Avatar of \${user.name}\`"&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 3. Boolean attributes: removed entirely when falsy --&gt;\n  &lt;button :disabled="!isActive"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 4. Spread an object of attributes --&gt;\n  &lt;section v-bind="attrs"&gt;…&lt;/section&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 5. Dynamic argument — the attribute NAME is an expression --&gt;\n  &lt;input :[isActive ? 'value' : 'placeholder']="user.name"&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 6. Events --&gt;\n  &lt;button @click="isActive = !isActive"&gt;Toggle&lt;/button&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 7. Raw HTML — bypasses escaping. ONLY for trusted content. --&gt;\n  &lt;div v-html="rawHtml"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- 8. Render literal braces without interpolation --&gt;\n  &lt;span v-pre&gt;{{ this is not compiled }}&lt;/span&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Escaping, demonstrated — the reason the distinction matters:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst comment = '&lt;img src=x onerror="steal(document.cookie)"&gt;'\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- ✅ Safe: renders the characters, executes nothing --&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ comment }}&lt;/p&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ❌ XSS: the onerror handler runs --&gt;\n  &lt;p v-html="comment"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Keeping logic out of templates — the readability rule that interviewers look for:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ❌ Unreadable, re-evaluated on every render, impossible to unit test --&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;{{ items.filter(i =&gt; i.active).map(i =&gt; i.price * (1 - i.discount)).reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, 0).toFixed(2) }}&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ✅ Named, cached by its dependencies, testable in isolation --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst activeTotal = computed(() =&gt;\n  items.value\n    .filter(i =&gt; i.active)\n    .reduce((sum, i) =&gt; sum + i.price * (1 - i.discount), 0)\n    .toFixed(2),\n)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;&lt;p&gt;{{ activeTotal }}&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Mustaches inside attributes.</strong> <code>&lt;a href="{{ url }}"&gt;</code> renders the literal braces. Use <code>:href="url"</code>. This is the single most common beginner error in Vue templates.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing statements in interpolation.</strong> Only expressions are allowed — no <code>if</code>, no <code>var</code>, no multi-statement bodies. Use a ternary, a computed property or a method.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling a method where a computed belongs.</strong> <code>{{ expensive() }}</code> re-runs on every single re-render; a <code>computed</code> re-runs only when its dependencies change.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-html</code> on user-supplied content.</strong> Straight XSS. Escape by default, sanitise (DOMPurify) if raw HTML is genuinely required, and never <code>v-html</code> anything a user can influence.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting globals in the sandbox.</strong> <code>window</code>, <code>document</code>, <code>localStorage</code> are not available to template expressions. Expose what you need from <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing <code>{{ count.value }}</code> in the template.</strong> Top-level refs are auto-unwrapped; adding <code>.value</code> yields <code>undefined</code>. (Refs nested inside a plain object are <em>not</em> unwrapped — see Chapter 4.)</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>:disabled="false"</code> leaves the attribute present.</strong> Vue removes boolean attributes entirely when the value is falsy, which is nearly always what you want but surprises people debugging in DevTools.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use the shorthands (<code>:</code>, <code>@</code>, <code>#</code>) consistently — the ecosystem does, and mixing forms adds noise.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep template expressions to a single, obvious operation. Anything longer belongs in a <code>computed</code> with a descriptive name.</li>`,
        `<li>Treat <code>{{ }}</code> as the default and <code>v-html</code> as an exception that needs justification — and sanitisation — at every site.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>computed</code> over method calls for anything derived and rendered, so the result is cached.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>v-bind="obj"</code> to forward a group of attributes rather than listing a dozen bindings by hand.</li>`,
        `<li>Reserve dynamic arguments (<code>:[key]="value"</code>) for genuinely dynamic cases; they defeat static analysis and make templates harder to read.</li>`,
        `<li>Let the template describe <em>structure</em> and the script own <em>logic</em>. A template that reads like HTML is the goal.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why can't you use <code>{{ }}</code> inside an attribute?"</strong><br>A: Because templates are compiled, not string-substituted. The compiler parses the template into an AST where text content and attributes are different node structures; mustaches are a text-node feature. To make an attribute dynamic you need a directive that tells the compiler to emit a binding, which is <code>v-bind</code> (<code>:</code>). Vue 1 allowed attribute mustaches; Vue 2 removed them for exactly this consistency.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the difference between <code>{{ }}</code> and <code>v-html</code>, and when is each safe?"</strong><br>A: <code>{{ }}</code> converts the value to a string and HTML-escapes it, so markup renders as visible text — safe with any input. <code>v-html</code> sets <code>innerHTML</code>, so markup is parsed and scripts, event handlers and iframes are live. It is safe only for content you fully control; on anything user-influenced it is a stored-XSS vulnerability. Sanitise with DOMPurify if you truly need rich HTML.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What can you put inside a template expression?"</strong><br>A: One JavaScript expression, evaluated against the component instance plus a small allow-list of globals. Ternaries, method calls, property access and chained array operations are fine. Declarations, <code>if</code>/<code>for</code> statements and arbitrary globals like <code>window</code> are not. If it needs more than one expression, it belongs in a computed or a method.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does template compilation make Vue faster than a naive virtual DOM?"</strong><br>A: Because a template is statically analysable, the compiler knows which nodes have no bindings and hoists them out of the render function so they are allocated once, not per render. For nodes that do have bindings, it emits patch flags describing exactly which aspects are dynamic, so the runtime diff checks only those and skips props, class and children it can prove are static. JSX cannot be optimised this way because it is arbitrary JavaScript.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does <code>{{ count }}</code> work but <code>{{ count.value }}</code> gives undefined?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Vue auto-unwraps top-level refs exposed to the template, so <code>count</code> already <em>is</em> the inner value and <code>.value</code> on a number is <code>undefined</code>. The unwrapping is shallow and applies to top-level bindings — a ref nested inside a plain object still needs <code>.value</code> in the template.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>{{ expr }}</code> — text position only, HTML-escaped, single expression.</li>`,
        `<li>Attributes need <code>v-bind</code> / <code>:</code>. Events need <code>v-on</code> / <code>@</code>. Slots use <code>#</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Templates compile to render functions → static hoisting + patch flags → fast diffs.</li>`,
        `<li>Expressions only: no declarations, no statements, no <code>window</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Refs auto-unwrap in templates — no <code>.value</code> (top level only).</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-html</code> bypasses escaping ⇒ XSS on untrusted input. Sanitise or don't use it.</li>`,
        `<li>Falsy boolean attributes (<code>:disabled="false"</code>) are removed from the DOM entirely.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-bind="obj"</code> spreads attributes; <code>:[key]="v"</code> is a dynamic attribute name; <code>v-pre</code> skips compilation.</li>`,
        `<li>Derived values → <code>computed</code>, never a long expression in the template.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Reactivity Basics: ref, reactive and the Mental Model',
      slug: 'reactivity-basics',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Reactivity is the heart of Vue. Every other feature — computed properties, watchers, component re-rendering, Pinia stores — is built on it. And it is the topic where interviews get real: "ref or reactive?" is asked in virtually every Vue 3 interview, and the follow-up ("why does destructuring break it?") separates people who have read the docs from people who have debugged the problem at 6pm on a Friday.</p>`,
        `<p>The practical stakes are high. Reactivity bugs do not throw. Nothing crashes, no red text appears in the console — the screen simply stops updating while the data underneath is perfectly correct. That silence is why understanding the mechanism, not just the API, is worth real time.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Reactive state is state Vue watches.</strong> When you read it during a render, Vue records "this component depends on this property." When you write to it, Vue looks up everything that recorded a dependency and re-runs it. That is the entire idea; the two APIs below are just two ways of creating such state.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>ref()</code></strong> wraps <em>any</em> value — primitive or object — in a box with a single <code>.value</code> property. Vue can intercept reads and writes of <code>.value</code> because it is a property on an object it owns.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const count = ref(0)\ncount.value++          // in script: .value required\n// in template: {{ count }} — auto-unwrapped</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong><code>reactive()</code></strong> takes an <em>object</em> and returns a Proxy of it. You use it exactly like the original object, with no <code>.value</code> — the Proxy intercepts every property access directly.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const state = reactive({ count: 0 })\nstate.count++          // no .value anywhere</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The obvious question is why both exist, and the answer explains the whole design: <strong>a Proxy can only wrap an object.</strong> You cannot proxy the number <code>0</code>. So for primitives Vue needs a container with a property to intercept — that container is the ref, and <code>.value</code> is the property. Once refs existed for primitives, extending them to objects too gave one API that covers every case, which is why the modern recommendation is "just use <code>ref</code>".</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch1-ref-vs-reactive.svg|Side-by-side diagram of ref boxing a primitive with a .value property intercepted by getters and setters, versus reactive wrapping an object in a Proxy intercepting property access directly|A ref boxes any value behind .value; reactive proxies an existing object. Only objects can be proxied — which is why refs exist.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Under the hood Vue runs a <strong>track / trigger</strong> loop. It is worth being able to describe it precisely, because that description <em>is</em> the answer to most follow-up questions.</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li>Rendering a component runs its render function inside a <strong>reactive effect</strong>. Vue sets a module-level variable, <code>activeEffect</code>, to that effect while it runs.</li>`,
        `<li>Any reactive property read during that run hits a Proxy <code>get</code> trap (or a ref's <code>value</code> getter), which calls <code>track()</code> — recording, in a global dependency map keyed by <em>object → property → set of effects</em>, that this effect depends on this property.</li>`,
        `<li>Writing to that property hits the <code>set</code> trap, which calls <code>trigger()</code> — looking up the set of effects for that exact property and scheduling them to re-run.</li>`,
        `<li>Scheduling is asynchronous and de-duplicated. Vue queues effects and flushes them on the next microtask, so changing ten properties in one function produces one re-render, not ten. That queue is what <code>nextTick()</code> lets you await.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `<p>Two consequences follow immediately, and both are heavily tested.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Consequence 1: reactivity attaches to the property access, not to the value.</strong> Tracking happens when you go <em>through</em> the proxy. Pull the value out and the connection is gone:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const state = reactive({ count: 0 })\nlet { count } = state   // ❌ copies the NUMBER 0 out of the proxy\ncount++                 // increments a plain local variable; nothing re-renders</code></pre>`,
        `<p>This is not a bug — it is a consequence of how JavaScript assignment works, and no framework using this technique can avoid it. Refs survive destructuring from an object because what you copy is the ref object itself, and <code>.value</code> still routes through its getter.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Consequence 2: <code>reactive()</code> returns a <em>different object</em> from the one you passed in.</strong> The original is untouched and un-tracked, so writes to it are invisible:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const raw = { count: 0 }\nconst state = reactive(raw)\nraw.count++            // ❌ bypasses the proxy — no trigger\nstate.count++          // ✅ goes through the proxy\nconsole.log(raw === state)  // false</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong>Deep by default.</strong> Both <code>ref</code> and <code>reactive</code> are deep: nested objects are converted to reactive proxies lazily, on first access, so <code>state.user.address.city = 'Pune'</code> triggers correctly. Lazy conversion means you do not pay to walk a large object you never read — a real improvement on Vue 2, which recursively walked everything at creation time. <code>shallowRef</code> and <code>shallowReactive</code> opt out when you are storing something large and immutable and only ever replace it wholesale.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Ref unwrapping</strong> has exactly three rules, and mixing them up is a classic interview trap:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>In <strong>templates</strong>: top-level refs unwrap automatically — <code>{{ count }}</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Inside a <strong><code>reactive</code> object</strong>: refs unwrap — <code>const s = reactive({ n: ref(0) }); s.n === 0</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Inside a plain <strong>array or Map</strong>: refs do <em>not</em> unwrap — <code>arr[0].value</code> is required.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, reactive, computed } from 'vue'\n\n// ── ref: works for anything ──\nconst count = ref(0)\nconst name = ref('Ada')\nconst user = ref({ id: 1, tags: ['admin'] })   // objects are fine too\n\n// ── reactive: objects only ──\nconst form = reactive({ email: '', password: '', remember: false })\n\nfunction submit() {\n  count.value++              // .value in script\n  user.value.tags.push('x')  // deep: nested array is reactive\n  form.email = ''            // no .value for reactive\n}\n\nconst summary = computed(() =&gt; \`\${name.value} clicked \${count.value}×\`)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- no .value anywhere in the template --&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ summary }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model="form.email"&gt;\n  &lt;button @click="submit"&gt;Go ({{ count }})&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The three ways reactivity silently dies, and their fixes:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { reactive, ref, toRefs, toRef } from 'vue'\n\nconst state = reactive({ count: 0, name: 'Ada' })\n\n// ❌ 1. Destructuring — copies values out of the proxy\nlet { count } = state\ncount++                       // dead\n\n// ✅ toRefs converts every property into a connected ref\nconst { count: c, name } = toRefs(state)\nc.value++                     // ✅ writes through to state.count\n\n// ✅ toRef for a single property\nconst justCount = toRef(state, 'count')\n\n// ❌ 2. Replacing a reactive object wholesale\nlet obj = reactive({ n: 1 })\nobj = { n: 2 }                // the variable now points at a plain object\n\n// ✅ Mutate it, or use a ref if you need to swap the whole value\nObject.assign(obj, { n: 2 })  // keeps the same proxy\nconst boxed = ref({ n: 1 })\nboxed.value = { n: 2 }        // ✅ replacing is exactly what refs are for\n\n// ❌ 3. Passing a primitive property to a function\nfunction useDouble(n) { return computed(() =&gt; n * 2) }\nuseDouble(state.count)        // receives the number 0, frozen forever\n\n// ✅ Pass the ref (or a getter) so the connection survives\nfunction useDouble2(nRef) { return computed(() =&gt; nRef.value * 2) }\nuseDouble2(toRef(state, 'count'))</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Ref unwrapping, demonstrated — the rule that catches people out:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const n = ref(0)\n\nconst obj = reactive({ n })   // inside reactive → unwrapped\nconsole.log(obj.n)            // 0   (not a ref)\nobj.n = 5                     // writes through to n.value\nconsole.log(n.value)          // 5\n\nconst arr = reactive([n])     // inside an array → NOT unwrapped\nconsole.log(arr[0].value)     // 5   (.value still required)\n\nconst map = reactive(new Map([['n', n]]))\nconsole.log(map.get('n').value) // 5  (same — collections don't unwrap)</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Destructuring a <code>reactive</code> object.</strong> The number-one reactivity bug. Values are copied out of the proxy and lose their connection. Use <code>toRefs()</code>, or use <code>ref</code> in the first place.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>.value</code> in script.</strong> <code>count++</code> on a ref produces <code>NaN</code> — you incremented the object, not the number. TypeScript and the Volar/Vue - Official extension catch this; use them.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing <code>.value</code> in the template.</strong> The opposite mistake. Top-level refs are already unwrapped, so <code>{{ count.value }}</code> is <code>undefined</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reassigning a <code>reactive</code> variable.</strong> <code>state = { … }</code> replaces the reference to the proxy; every existing dependency still points at the old object. Mutate with <code>Object.assign</code>, or use a <code>ref</code> when the whole value needs replacing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating the original object passed to <code>reactive()</code>.</strong> The raw object is not tracked; only the returned proxy is. Never keep a reference to the original.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>reactive()</code> to work on a primitive.</strong> <code>reactive(0)</code> returns the primitive unchanged and, in development, warns. Proxies only wrap objects.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading a ref in a plain array and forgetting <code>.value</code>.</strong> Unwrapping applies to templates and <code>reactive</code> objects, not to arrays, Maps or Sets.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming reactivity is synchronous.</strong> The DOM updates on the next microtask. Reading <code>el.textContent</code> immediately after a state change gives the old value; <code>await nextTick()</code> first.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Default to <code>ref()</code>.</strong> It handles every type, survives destructuring and reassignment, and gives you one consistent rule instead of two. This is the official recommendation.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>reactive()</code> for a cohesive group of fields mutated together — a form object is the classic fit — where the absence of <code>.value</code> genuinely reads better.</li>`,
        `<li>Never mix both for the same piece of state. Pick one per concern and stay consistent.</li>`,
        `<li>Return refs from composables, not a <code>reactive</code> object — consumers will destructure, and refs survive it.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for <code>toRefs()</code> at the boundary where a reactive object must be spread or destructured.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>shallowRef</code> for large immutable payloads you always replace wholesale (a parsed dataset, a chart config) — deep conversion of a 10,000-row array costs real time.</li>`,
        `<li>Let TypeScript help: <code>ref&lt;User | null&gt;(null)</code> documents intent and catches missing <code>.value</code> at compile time.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "ref vs reactive — what's the difference and which do you use?"</strong><br>A: <code>ref</code> boxes any value behind <code>.value</code>; <code>reactive</code> returns a Proxy of an object and needs no <code>.value</code>. <code>reactive</code> works only on objects, breaks when destructured and cannot be reassigned; <code>ref</code> has none of those limits. I default to <code>ref</code> everywhere and use <code>reactive</code> only for a tightly grouped set of fields like a form, where dropping <code>.value</code> reads better.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does <code>ref</code> need <code>.value</code> at all?"</strong><br>A: Vue 3 tracks dependencies with <code>Proxy</code>, and a Proxy can only wrap an object — you cannot proxy the number <code>0</code>. So a ref is a container object with a single <code>value</code> property whose getter and setter Vue can intercept. Objects get the same wrapper for API consistency; templates auto-unwrap top-level refs so the ergonomic cost lands only in script.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Explain what happens internally when I write <code>count.value++</code>."</strong><br>A: The setter fires and calls <code>trigger()</code>, which looks up the dependency map for this ref and finds every effect that read it. Those effects — the component's render effect, any computed that depends on it, any watcher — are pushed onto a de-duplicated job queue and flushed on the next microtask. When the render effect re-runs it produces a new virtual DOM tree, which is diffed against the old one and patched into the real DOM.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "This code doesn't update the UI. Why?" — <code>const { count } = reactive({ count: 0 }); count++</code> (trap)</strong><br>A: Destructuring reads <code>count</code> through the proxy once and copies the primitive <code>0</code> into a new local binding. From then on you are incrementing a plain variable with no connection to the proxy, so no <code>set</code> trap fires and nothing re-renders. Fix with <code>toRefs()</code>, or use <code>ref</code> from the start. Nothing throws — the UI just silently stops updating, which is why this bug is so common.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How is Vue 3's reactivity better than Vue 2's?"</strong><br>A: Vue 2 used <code>Object.defineProperty</code> per property, which meant it had to walk the entire object at creation time, could not detect properties added or deleted later (hence <code>Vue.set</code>/<code>Vue.delete</code>), could not intercept array index assignment or <code>length</code>, and could not track Map or Set. <code>Proxy</code> intercepts the operations themselves, so additions, deletions, array indices and collections all work, conversion is lazy so large objects cost nothing until read, and the workarounds disappear. The trade-off is that <code>Proxy</code> cannot be polyfilled, which is why Vue 3 dropped IE11.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When do refs auto-unwrap?"</strong><br>A: Three rules. Top-level refs exposed to a template unwrap. Refs used as properties of a <code>reactive</code> object unwrap, and writing to that property writes through to the ref. Refs inside plain arrays, Maps or Sets do <em>not</em> unwrap — you still need <code>.value</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>ref(any)</code> → <code>.value</code> in script, auto-unwrapped in template. Works for every type.</li>`,
        `<li><code>reactive(obj)</code> → Proxy, no <code>.value</code>. Objects only. Cannot be reassigned or destructured.</li>`,
        `<li>Proxies can't wrap primitives — that is the whole reason refs exist.</li>`,
        `<li>Mechanism: read → <code>track()</code> into a target→key→effects map; write → <code>trigger()</code> → queue → flush on the next microtask.</li>`,
        `<li>Updates are batched and async ⇒ <code>await nextTick()</code> before reading the DOM.</li>`,
        `<li>Both are <strong>deep</strong> and convert lazily. <code>shallowRef</code>/<code>shallowReactive</code> opt out.</li>`,
        `<li>Destructuring <code>reactive</code> kills reactivity → <code>toRefs()</code> / <code>toRef()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>reactive(raw) !== raw</code> — mutating <code>raw</code> triggers nothing.</li>`,
        `<li>Unwrapping: templates ✅ · inside <code>reactive</code> ✅ · inside arrays/Maps ❌.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Default to <code>ref</code>.</strong></li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Single-File Components and Project Structure',
      slug: 'sfc-project-structure',
      order: 4,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>The Single-File Component is the format you will spend your entire Vue career writing, and it is one of the framework's most-copied ideas. But interviewers rarely ask "what is an SFC?" — they ask the questions underneath it. How does <code>scoped</code> actually scope CSS, and what are its limits? Why does <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> exist when <code>setup()</code> already did the job? How does the browser run a <code>.vue</code> file at all?</p>`,
        `<p>Those questions matter on the job because the answers determine whether your styles leak into a child component, whether your component works in a library build, and how quickly a new developer can find anything in your repository. Structure is not bikeshedding: on a codebase with four hundred components, a predictable layout is the difference between a ten-second search and a ten-minute one.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>Single-File Component</strong> is a <code>.vue</code> file containing up to three top-level blocks, each owning one concern:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;template&gt;</code> — the markup. One per file.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> — the logic. State, computeds, functions, imports.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;style&gt;</code> — the CSS. Optional, repeatable, and <code>scoped</code> when you want it confined to this component.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>The rationale is <strong>separation of concerns by component, not by file type</strong>. The traditional split — all HTML in one folder, all CSS in another, all JS in a third — separates technologies, not concerns. A button's markup, behaviour and appearance are one concern; splitting them across three directories means every change to the button touches three places. The SFC keeps what changes together, together.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch1-sfc-anatomy.svg|Anatomy of a .vue Single-File Component showing template, script setup and scoped style blocks, and the compiled output of render function, setup function and scoped CSS with data attributes|One file, three blocks, one concern — compiled at build time into a render function, a setup function and scoped CSS.}}`,
        `<p>Browsers cannot execute <code>.vue</code> files. A build step — Vite in practice — compiles each one into a standard JavaScript module: the template becomes a render function, the script becomes a component definition, and the styles are extracted into a CSS file with the scoping transformation applied.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code></strong> is compile-time sugar over the <code>setup()</code> function, and understanding what it compiles <em>to</em> answers most questions about it. The whole block becomes the body of a <code>setup()</code> function, and every top-level binding — variables, functions, imports — is automatically exposed to the template. That is why there is no <code>return</code> statement and why importing a component is enough to use it: the import <em>is</em> a top-level binding.</p>`,
        `<p>Because it is a compiler transform rather than a runtime feature, it also runs faster: bindings are exposed by direct reference in the compiled render function instead of through a returned object, and the compiler can inline them. Three macros exist only inside this block and are compiled away rather than imported: <code>defineProps</code>, <code>defineEmits</code> and <code>defineExpose</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;style scoped&gt;</code></strong> is not the Shadow DOM and does not use it. At build time Vue assigns the component a unique hash — <code>data-v-7ba5bd90</code> — stamps it as an attribute on every element the component renders, and rewrites each selector in the block to require it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-css">/* you write */      .title { color: red }\n/* Vue emits */      .title[data-v-7ba5bd90] { color: red }</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Two consequences follow, and both are asked about. First, this is plain CSS specificity, not isolation: a global <code>.title { color: blue !important }</code> still wins. Second, a child component's <strong>root element</strong> carries both the parent's and its own hash, deliberately, so a parent can lay out its children. Everything <em>inside</em> the child is untouchable from the parent unless you use the <code>:deep()</code> pseudo-class — which exists precisely for styling third-party component internals.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Other style features</strong> worth knowing: <code>&lt;style module&gt;</code> gives CSS Modules with a <code>$style</code> object instead of attribute scoping; <code>v-bind()</code> inside CSS binds a reactive value straight into a custom property, so <code>color: v-bind(textColor)</code> updates live; and a file may contain several <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> blocks with different attributes.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Project structure.</strong> The convention produced by <code>npm create vue@latest</code> is worth following simply because every Vue developer already knows it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-text">src/\n├── assets/          images, fonts, global CSS\n├── components/      reusable, presentational components\n│   ├── base/        design-system primitives (BaseButton.vue…)\n│   └── ...\n├── composables/     reusable logic — useAuth.js, useFetch.js\n├── views/           route-level components (one per page)\n├── router/          route definitions and guards\n├── stores/          Pinia stores\n├── services/        API clients, HTTP wrappers\n├── utils/           pure helpers, no Vue imports\n├── App.vue          root component\n└── main.js          createApp → configure → mount</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The distinction that matters most is <strong><code>views/</code> vs <code>components/</code></strong>: a view is mapped to a route and may fetch data and read route params; a component is reusable, takes props and emits events, and does not know it is on a page. Blurring that line is how you end up with a "component" that cannot be reused because it reads <code>route.params.id</code> internally.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- components/UserCard.vue --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { computed } from 'vue'\nimport BaseAvatar from './base/BaseAvatar.vue'  // importing = usable, no registration\n\nconst props = defineProps({\n  user: { type: Object, required: true },\n  compact: { type: Boolean, default: false },\n})\nconst emit = defineEmits(['select'])\n\nconst initials = computed(() =&gt;\n  props.user.name.split(' ').map(w =&gt; w[0]).join('').toUpperCase(),\n)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;article class="card" :class="{ 'card--compact': compact }" @click="emit('select', user.id)"&gt;\n    &lt;BaseAvatar :initials="initials" /&gt;\n    &lt;h3 class="title"&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;p class="meta"&gt;{{ user.role }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/article&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;style scoped&gt;\n.card  { display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; padding: 1rem; }\n.title { font-weight: 600; color: v-bind('user.themeColor'); }  /* reactive CSS */\n.card--compact { padding: 0.5rem; }\n\n/* Reach into a child component's internals — use sparingly */\n:deep(.avatar-ring) { border-color: currentColor; }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>How scoping behaves at the boundary between parent and child — the part people get wrong:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Parent.vue --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div class="wrapper"&gt;\n    &lt;ChildCard class="child" /&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;style scoped&gt;\n.wrapper { padding: 2rem }        /* ✅ own element */\n.child   { margin-bottom: 1rem }  /* ✅ child's ROOT gets the parent's hash too */\n.child .inner-title { color: red } /* ❌ .inner-title is inside the child — no hash match */\n:deep(.inner-title) { color: red } /* ✅ explicit opt-in to pierce the boundary */\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> versus the older forms, so you can read any codebase:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Modern: script setup — recommended --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nconst count = ref(0)                    // exposed automatically\nfunction inc() { count.value++ }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Composition API without the sugar — note the explicit return --&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nexport default {\n  setup() {\n    const count = ref(0)\n    function inc() { count.value++ }\n    return { count, inc }               // forget this and the template sees nothing\n  },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Options API — still fully supported --&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nexport default {\n  data() { return { count: 0 } },\n  methods: { inc() { this.count++ } },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>When you need both — a normal <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block can coexist with <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> for things that must run once at module scope:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script&gt;\n// runs ONCE on import, not per component instance\nexport default { inheritAttrs: false }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;script setup&gt;\n// runs per instance\nconst open = ref(false)\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Believing <code>scoped</code> is true isolation.</strong> It is an attribute selector, so global styles with higher specificity still win, and a child's root element intentionally receives the parent's hash.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Trying to style a child's internals from a scoped parent block.</strong> The elements never get the parent's hash. Use <code>:deep()</code> — and prefer exposing a prop or a CSS custom property if you own the child.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the <code>return</code> in a plain <code>setup()</code>.</strong> The template renders nothing and no error is thrown. <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> removes this failure mode entirely.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Importing <code>defineProps</code>/<code>defineEmits</code>.</strong> They are compiler macros, not runtime functions — importing them is unnecessary and, in older versions, a build warning.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using the macros outside <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>.</strong> They only exist inside that block; in a plain <code>setup()</code> you declare <code>props</code>/<code>emits</code> as options.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Non-scoped styles in a shared component.</strong> One unscoped <code>.button { … }</code> in a widely imported component restyles the whole application.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting route-aware logic in <code>components/</code>.</strong> A component that reads <code>useRoute()</code> is a view; it cannot be reused anywhere else on the page.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Two root-level <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> blocks.</strong> One per file. Multiple root <em>elements</em> inside that template are fine in Vue 3 (fragments) — that was the Vue 2 restriction.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> for every new component — less boilerplate, better inference, faster compiled output.</li>`,
        `<li>Name component files in <code>PascalCase.vue</code> and use multi-word names (<code>UserCard</code>, not <code>Card</code>) to avoid collisions with current and future HTML elements.</li>`,
        `<li>Scope every style block by default. Drop <code>scoped</code> deliberately, in a single global stylesheet, not scattered around.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer a documented prop or a CSS custom property over <code>:deep()</code> when you control the child — <code>:deep()</code> couples you to someone else's internal DOM, which is not part of their API.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep <code>views/</code> for routed pages and <code>components/</code> for reusable pieces, and enforce the split in review.</li>`,
        `<li>Extract reusable logic into <code>composables/</code> the moment two components need it; that is what the Composition API is for.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep components small enough to read in one screen. A file past ~300 lines is usually two components or a component plus a composable.</li>`,
        `<li>Order blocks consistently — <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> — and let ESLint's <code>vue/component-tags-order</code> enforce it.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does <code>&lt;style scoped&gt;</code> work?"</strong><br>A: At build time Vue generates a hash for the component, adds it as a data attribute (<code>data-v-abc123</code>) to every element the component renders, and rewrites each selector to include an attribute selector for it. So it is CSS specificity, not isolation — global styles can still override it, there is no Shadow DOM involved, and a child component's root element deliberately carries the parent's hash so the parent can position it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> actually do?"</strong><br>A: It is a compile-time transform. The block's contents become the body of a <code>setup()</code> function and every top-level binding is exposed to the template automatically, so there is no return statement and importing a component is enough to use it. Because the compiler knows the bindings, it can reference them directly in the generated render function rather than going through a returned object, which is both less code and slightly faster. It also enables the <code>defineProps</code>/<code>defineEmits</code>/<code>defineExpose</code> macros.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "My parent's scoped style won't apply to something inside a child component. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Scoped selectors are rewritten to require the parent's hash attribute, and elements rendered by the child only carry the child's hash. The one exception is the child's root element, which gets both. To reach deeper, use <code>:deep(.selector)</code> — but that couples you to the child's internal DOM, so if you own the child, expose a prop or a CSS variable instead.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why single-file components — isn't that mixing concerns?"</strong><br>A: It separates concerns by <em>component</em> rather than by file type. A button's markup, behaviour and styles are one concern that changes together; splitting them across three directories means every change touches three files and nothing is co-located. The blocks are still distinct within the file, so you keep the separation without the scatter — and the compiler gets to see all three together, which is how <code>v-bind()</code> in CSS and scoped styles are possible at all.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you decide what goes in <code>views/</code> versus <code>components/</code>?"</strong><br>A: A view is a route target: it can read route params, fetch data and compose the page. A component is reusable and route-agnostic: it takes props, emits events and could appear anywhere. The test is whether you could drop it on a second page unchanged — if it calls <code>useRoute()</code> internally, you cannot, so it is a view.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>SFC = <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> (one) + <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> + <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> (many), compiled by Vite.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> = compiler sugar over <code>setup()</code>; top-level bindings auto-exposed, no <code>return</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Macros <code>defineProps</code> / <code>defineEmits</code> / <code>defineExpose</code> — compiled away, never imported.</li>`,
        `<li><code>scoped</code> = hash attribute + rewritten selectors. Specificity, not isolation. No Shadow DOM.</li>`,
        `<li>Child's <strong>root</strong> element carries the parent's hash; everything deeper needs <code>:deep()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;style module&gt;</code> → CSS Modules via <code>$style</code>; <code>v-bind()</code> in CSS → reactive custom property.</li>`,
        `<li>Structure: <code>components/</code> reusable · <code>views/</code> routed · <code>composables/</code> logic · <code>stores/</code> Pinia · <code>services/</code> API.</li>`,
        `<li>PascalCase, multi-word file names. Vue 3 allows multiple root elements (fragments).</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
